ecords/catalog_files/pcc_bibs.mrc_rev.mrc:77814982:1014","ia:basicwritings00zhua","marc:marc_openlibraries_sanfranciscopubliclibrary/sfpl_chq_2018_12_24_run01.mrc:48943269:1371","marc:marc_claremont_school_theology/CSTMARC1_barcode.mrc:36197479:2760","bwb:9780231086066","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part25.utf8:157858753:753","marc:marc_claremont_school_theology/CSTMARC1_multibarcode.mrc:36225731:2760","marc:marc_gtu/KLF IA Export 5-5-22.mrc:11604680:902","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-005.mrc:167027614:2936","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-002.mrc:615399566:1783","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-010.mrc:271897805:1067","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_067.bib.mrc:174993595:3154","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_035.bib.mrc:308179459:3090"],"title":"Chuang Tzu","work_titles":["Nan-hua Ching."],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes index."},"number_of_pages":148,"languages":[{"key":"/languages/eng"}],"dewey_decimal_class":["181.11"],"local_id":["urn:sfpl:31223037735231","urn:sfpl:31223063585286","urn:cst:10011337925","urn:gtu:32400001869019"],"publish_date":"1964","publish_country":"nyu","by_statement":"translated by Burton Watson.","oclc_numbers":["30268950","1049751866","393063","34797390"],"works":[{"key":"/works/OL9425814W"}],"type":{"key":"/type/edition"},"description":{"type":"/type/text","value":"The basic writings of Chuang Tzu have been savored by Chinese readers for more than two thousand years. And Burton Watson's lucid and beautiful translation has been loved by generations of readers.\n\nChuang Tzu (369?-286? B.C.) was a leading philosopher representing the Taoist strain in Chinese thought. Using parable and anecdote, allegory and paradox, he set forth, in the book that bears his name, the early ideas of what was to become the Taoist school. Central to these is the belief that only by understanding Tao (the Way of Nature) and dwelling in its unity can man achieve true happiness and freedom, in both life and death.\n\nChuang Tzu: Basic Writings includes the seven \"inner chapters,\" which form the heart of the book, three of the \"outer chapters,\" and one of the \"miscellaneous chapters.\" Watson also provides an introduction, which places the philosopher in relation to Chinese history and thought.\n\nWitty and imaginative, enriched by brilliant imagery, and making sportive use of both mythological and historical personages (including even Confucius), this timeless classic is sure to appeal to anyone interested in Chinese religion and culture."},"latest_revision":19,"revision":19,"created":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2008-10-16T08:53:15.805984"},"last_modified":{"type":"/type/datetime","value":"2026-04-25T03:50:46.657840"}}},"ISBN:9780231118392":{"bib_key":"ISBN:9780231118392","info_url":"http://openlibrary.org/books/OL15501805M/Po_Chu-i","preview":"restricted","preview_url":"https://archive.org/details/pochuiselectedpo0000baij","thumbnail_url":"https://covers.openlibrary.org/b/id/14345729-S.jpg","details":{"publishers":["Columbia University Press"],"number_of_pages":172,"subtitle":"selected poems","isbn_10":["0231118384","0231118392"],"series":["Translations from the Asian classics"],"lc_classifications":["PL2674 .A28 2000","PL2674.A28 2000"],"key":"/books/OL15501805M","authors":[{"key":"/authors/OL19722A","name":"Bai, Juyi"}],"publish_places":["New York"],"contributions":["Watson, Burton, 1925-"],"pagination":"xvi, 172 p. ;","source_records":["marc:marc_western_washington_univ/wwu_bibs.mrc_revrev.mrc:744645477:961","marc:marc_loc_2016/BooksAll.2016.part27.utf8:232755757:893","bwb:9780231118385","bwb:9780231118392","ia:pochuiselectedpo0000baij","marc:marc_columbia/Columbia-extract-20221130-005.mrc:567205276:1447","marc:harvard_bibliographic_metadata/20220215_046.bib.mrc:73663133:2241"],"title":"Po Chu-i","work_titles":["Poems."],"notes":{"type":"/type/text","value":"Includes bibliographical references (p. [163])."},"identifiers"